{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: stop refusing a stale read as a frontier rollback.\n\nWhy. On 2026-09-17 gs land, gs ratify, gs review-request and the docset anchor gate each failed with \"refuse verified frontier rollback: depth N is shorter than previously verified depth N+1\" (for example depth 23487 against 23489), and every retry succeeded. The mechanism is in internal/app/attach.go checkVerifiedFrontier, called from Workspace.rememberVerifiedFrontier: a command reads and verifies the sequence at depth N, which takes minutes at this depth, and during that time the resident on the same checkout appends and advances the persisted witness to N+1; the finished read is then judged shorter than the witness and refused. The witness exists to refuse a sequence ref that moved backwards; a read that finished after the world moved on is not that. mergeVerifiedFrontier already keeps the deeper frontier without error in the config path, so the transaction path is the inconsistent one. Each false refusal costs the actor a full re-verification.\n\nOutcome. A verification shorter than the witness is admitted without advancing the witness when, inside the same config transaction, the sequence ref at judgement time still continues the witnessed head, because the read was merely stale. It is refused, as now, when the ref itself no longer reaches the witnessed head, because that is a rollback. The result returned to the caller is the older verified snapshot, which the fold and the resident re-judge on append as they do today.","body":{"branch":"request/frontier-stale-read","conditions":"1. checkVerifiedFrontier (or its replacement) distinguishes a stale read, where the current sequence ref continues the stored witness head, from a rollback, where it does not, and admits the first without moving the witness; the same rule applies on the attach path. 2. Tests pin both: a verification at depth N against a witness at N+1 with the ref at N+1 returns the depth-N snapshot and leaves the witness at N+1; the same with the ref reset to N refuses with the rollback message; a verification whose head is not an ancestor of the witness refuses. 3. A concurrency test drives a snapshot read while another appender advances the ref and the witness, and shows the read completes without refusal. 4. docs/reference/architecture.md or the page that describes the rollback witness states the rule. 5. go test ./... green at the exact head; independent review by codex, whose security conclusion addresses the rollback guarantee explicitly; ratified; landed on main and pushed.","target_head":"3c7f13c79cb079ef0f40961d51d7ff4f350787c5","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}